C = [
("h1", "14. Discipline Beyond Corinth"),
("p", "Claim: Paul’s other passages on discipline each give their own aim, and the aims are restoration, shame, warning and rejection. They are not one procedure. The volume reads them in order and leaves Matthew 18 in its own setting."),
("sub", "14.1 The man who was punished: 2 Corinthians 2:5–11"),
("p", "Paul writes later of one who has “caused grief.” “Sufficient to such a man is this punishment, which was inflicted of many. So that contrariwise ye ought rather to forgive him, and comfort him, lest perhaps such a one should be swallowed up with overmuch sorrow. Wherefore I beseech you that ye would confirm your love toward him” (2 Corinthians 2:6–8). The aims are stated: forgiveness, comfort and love, and the reason is “Lest Satan should get an advantage of us: for we are not ignorant of his devices” (2 Corinthians 2:11). The passage says that the punishment was “inflicted of many,” which is the church’s act. It does not name the man or say that he is the man of 1 Corinthians 5. The volume notes that Paul does not say it and does not rest on it."),
("sub", "14.2 The brother overtaken: Galatians 6:1"),
("p", "“Brethren, if a man be overtaken in a fault, ye which are spiritual, restore such an one in the spirit of meekness; considering thyself, lest thou also be tempted” (Galatians 6:1). The instruction is to “restore,” and it is given to “ye which are spiritual,” in “the spirit of meekness.” The ground stated is the restorer’s own case: “lest thou also be tempted.” There is no mention of putting away. The case is of a man who is overtaken, which is a different case from the one in 1 Corinthians 5."),
("sub", "14.3 The disorderly: 2 Thessalonians 3:6–15"),
("p", "Paul commands in the name of the Lord: “that ye withdraw yourselves from every brother that walketh disorderly, and not after the tradition which he received of us” (2 Thessalonians 3:6). The disorder is described: some are “working not at all, but are busybodies” (2 Thessalonians 3:11). He appeals to his own example (3:7–9) and to the rule he gave: “if any would not work, neither should he eat” (3:10). The discipline is then given: “And if any man obey not our word by this epistle, note that man, and have no company with him, that he may be ashamed. Yet count him not as an enemy, but admonish him as a brother” (2 Thessalonians 3:14, 15). Two aims are stated in the verses, shame and admonition, and the limit is that he is not counted “as an enemy” but remains “a brother.”"),
("sub", "14.4 The elder who sins: 1 Timothy 5:19, 20"),
("p", "“Against an elder receive not an accusation, but before two or three witnesses. Them that sin rebuke before all, that others also may fear” (1 Timothy 5:19, 20). The first sentence is about the elder and protects him from a single accuser. The second is about those who sin, and its aim is stated: “that others also may fear.” It says “before all,” and the volume does not say whether “all” means the whole church or a wider company. Paul charges Timothy to do it “without preferring one before another, doing nothing by partiality” (1 Timothy 5:21)."),
("sub", "14.5 The heretick: Titus 3:10, 11"),
("p", "“A man that is an heretick after the first and second admonition reject; Knowing that he that is such is subverted, and sinneth, being condemned of himself” (Titus 3:10, 11). The procedure is two admonitions and then rejection, and the ground is that the man “is subverted” and “condemned of himself.” The passage is addressed to Titus, a delegate, and the volume keeps that in view. It says nothing here of a church’s vote or of an officer’s pronouncement."),
("sub", "14.6 Matthew 18 left in its setting"),
("p", "Matthew 18 has a well-known procedure: “if thy brother shall trespass against thee, go and tell him his fault between thee and him alone” (Matthew 18:15), then one or two more, and “if he shall neglect to hear them, tell it unto the church” (Matthew 18:17). The volume does not read the passage as Paul’s order for the Body’s discipline. It stands in the Lord’s ministry to Israel in the Gospel, where the word “church” and the people addressed are read in their own setting, as the Digital Study on the churches named in Scripture does (Study 60). Paul’s passages give their own aims and procedures, and none of them cites Matthew 18. The volume therefore reads Paul’s passages on their own grounds, and it does not join them to the Gospel’s."),
("ctbl", "Paul’s passages on discipline and their stated aims.", [
    ("Passage", "The case", "The action, and the aim stated"),
    ("1 Corinthians 5", "A brother with his father’s wife", "Put away; that the spirit may be saved, and that the leaven not spread"),
    ("2 Corinthians 2:5–11", "One who had caused grief and been punished by many", "Forgive and comfort; lest Satan get an advantage"),
    ("Galatians 6:1", "A man overtaken in a fault", "Restore in the spirit of meekness; lest thou also be tempted"),
    ("2 Thessalonians 3:6–15", "A brother who walks disorderly and obeys not Paul’s word", "Note him; no company; that he may be ashamed; admonish as a brother"),
    ("1 Timothy 5:19, 20", "An elder accused; those who sin", "Two or three witnesses; rebuke before all; that others may fear"),
    ("Titus 3:10, 11", "A heretick", "Reject after two admonitions; he is subverted and condemned of himself"),
  ], [0.22, 0.34, 0.44]),
("sub", "14.7 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Paul’s passages on discipline each give an aim of their own: restoration, shame, warning and rejection.",
    "They differ in the case, the actor and the action, and the volume does not make them one procedure.",
    "In 2 Thessalonians 3 the brother is not counted as an enemy; where a man is rejected, in Titus 3, the ground is stated.",
    "Matthew 18 is left in its setting and is not read as Paul’s order for the Body.",
  ]),
]
